  "slug": "nanopore-ionic-5535",
  "summary": "Proteome-scale inference is now tested for direct partnership and mapped with embedding and graph measures across binding sites and regulatory networks.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread across these items is inferring molecular relationships from sequence, structure, or network data rather than direct measurement. A relation-aware graph-hypergraph network with SE(3)-equivariant encoders addresses antibody CDR sequence-structure co-design and reports gains over the compared approaches. A binding-site study pairs ProtT5 embeddings of ligand-contacting residues with mutual nearest-neighbor networks across 121,673 binding sites to map multiscale organization. A separate preprint introduces antagonistic-cooperative polarization, a target-wise sign principle with a node-separable polarization index, applied through null-model analysis to reconstructed pathway-associated regulatory networks. What is new is the extension of network inference from physical to regulatory interactions, paired with explicit structural constraints and null-model testing. The items are heterogeneous, so this thread is dominant rather than shared by every item; the antibody co-design work is primarily a structural modeling method.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-21"
}
